        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. The Short Answer: A Water Heater With No Tank
          </h2>

          <p>
            A conventional water heater keeps 40 or 50 gallons hot around the clock, reheating it every time it cools, whether anyone needs it or not. A tankless water heater, also sold as an on-demand, instant or instantaneous water heater, stores nothing. Cold water enters, passes through a compact heat exchanger, and leaves hot. When the tap closes, the heater shuts down and goes back to idling on a few watts of standby power for its electronics.
          </p>

          <p>
            Because there is no stored volume to run out, a tankless heater can supply hot water indefinitely. A fourth back-to-back shower comes out exactly as hot as the first. What it cannot do is supply unlimited hot water at the same moment. Its output is measured in gallons per minute, and every fixture you open draws from the same fixed budget. Endless in duration, finite in rate: that is the whole trade in one line.
          </p>

          <p>
            The idea is not new. Gas tankless heaters have been the default in Japan and much of Europe for decades, where small homes and expensive energy made a 50-gallon tank in the basement an odd choice. In North America they spread more slowly, mostly because a big gas or electric tankless unit needs far more fuel or power at its peak than a tank does, and older houses were not plumbed or wired for that.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you are still working out which family of heater suits your home, our guide to the <a href="/types-of-water-heaters/">types of water heaters</a> sets tankless beside tank, heat pump, solar and combination systems. This page stays on the tankless unit itself: what is inside it, how it decides how hard to fire, and which numbers on the spec sheet actually predict whether your showers stay hot in February.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE ONE-LINE DEFINITION</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">A tankless heater trades storage for firepower.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Instead of keeping a reserve of hot water, it heats water at the moment of use with a burner or elements several times more powerful than a tank's. You never run out, but you can only draw so many gallons per minute at once.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="open-the-tap" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. What Happens Inside When You Open the Tap
          </h2>

          <p>
            Everything starts with a flow sensor on the cold-water inlet, usually a small turbine that spins as water moves past it. When you open a hot tap, water starts to flow through the heater, the turbine spins, and the control board reads the flow rate. Nothing happens until that flow crosses the unit's activation threshold, which on most whole-house gas models is somewhere around 0.4 to 0.5 gallons per minute, and lower on some newer designs.
          </p>

          <p>
            On a gas unit, the board then runs a short ignition sequence. A combustion fan starts and purges the chamber, the gas valve opens, an igniter lights the burner and a flame sensor confirms it is lit. On an electric unit there is no ignition at all; the board simply switches power to a set of resistance elements wrapped around or immersed in the water path. Either way, heating starts within a few seconds of the tap opening.
          </p>

          <p>
            The water then passes through the heat exchanger, a coil of copper or stainless tubing with a lot of surface area packed into a small space. The burner's hot combustion gases, or the elements, transfer heat into the water as it travels through. Because the water is only inside the heater for a few seconds, the heat source has to be very powerful: a large gas tankless unit is typically rated at up to 199,000 BTU per hour, roughly four to five times the burner on a typical 40-gallon gas tank.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, a temperature sensor on the outlet tells the board how hot the water leaving the unit actually is. The board compares that to your set point and continuously adjusts the gas valve or the element power to hold it there. When you close the tap, flow drops below the shut-off threshold, the burner or elements cut out, and the fan runs briefly to clear the chamber before the unit goes quiet again.
          </p>

