        <div id="flow-and-rise" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Flow Rate and Temperature Rise: The Two Numbers That Rule It
          </h2>

          <p>
            Every tankless water heater has a fixed maximum heat output. How many gallons per minute it can deliver depends on how much that water has to be heated, which is called the temperature rise. Rise is simply your set point minus the temperature of the water coming in from the street or well. Heating 50-degree water to 120 degrees is a 70-degree rise; heating 70-degree water to the same set point is only a 50-degree rise.
          </p>

          <p>
            That is why the big number on the box is almost never your number. A unit advertised at 11 GPM reaches that figure only at a small rise, usually in a warm climate. The standard rule of thumb is that it takes about 500 BTU per hour to raise one gallon per minute by one degree. A 199,000 BTU unit at around 95 percent efficiency therefore gives roughly 5.4 GPM at a 70-degree rise, but closer to 8.4 GPM at a 45-degree rise.
          </p>

          <p>
            Groundwater temperature changes with season and region. Northern states often see incoming water in the high 30s or low 40s in winter, while the Gulf Coast and Southwest rarely drop below the 60s. The same heater can comfortably run two showers in Phoenix and struggle with one shower plus a dishwasher in Minneapolis in January. You size a tankless heater for your coldest month, not your average one.
          </p>

          <p>
            When demand exceeds what the heater can handle, it does not run out the way a tank does. Most units either let the outlet temperature sag, or restrict flow with an internal valve to protect the set point, so the shower gets weaker rather than colder. Our step-by-step guide to <a href="/what-size-tankless-water-heater-do-i-need/">what size tankless water heater you need</a> walks through adding up fixture flow rates and finding your winter rise so you can do this math for your own house.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">Typical Figures</p>
