                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">GPM to activate (typical gas)</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">199k</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">BTU/h on large gas units</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">~500</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">BTU/h per GPM per °F</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">5-6</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">GPM at a 70°F rise, 199k gas</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="gas-vs-electric" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Gas vs Electric Tankless: Same Idea, Very Different Limits
          </h2>

          <p>
            Gas tankless heaters, whether natural gas or propane, are the whole-house workhorses. Their burners deliver far more heat than any practical residential electric unit, so they can supply several fixtures at once even in cold climates. The catch is everything around the unit: a burner that size usually needs a larger gas line than an old tank used, a dedicated vent run to the outside, a 120-volt outlet for the fan and electronics, and in condensing models a drain for condensate.
          </p>

          <p>
            Electric tankless heaters have no flame, no vent and very few moving parts, which makes them simple to place and quiet to run. Their limit is the electrical service. One kilowatt equals about 3,412 BTU per hour, so even a large 27 kW electric unit produces around 92,000 BTU, less than half a big gas model. At a 70-degree rise that works out to roughly 2.5 to 3 GPM, which is about one shower in a cold-climate winter.
          </p>

          <p>
            That 27 kW unit also draws around 112 amps at 240 volts, typically spread across three double-pole 40-amp breakers with heavy-gauge wire. Many homes with 100-amp or even 150-amp service simply cannot add that load without a panel upgrade. In warm climates where incoming water is 65 degrees or more, though, an electric whole-house unit can work well, and it avoids the venting and gas-line costs entirely.
          </p>

          <p>
            The practical rule is that gas suits whole-house tankless in most of the country, and electric suits warm climates, small homes and single fixtures. Our <a href="/best-electric-tankless-water-heater/">electric tankless water heater picks</a> are sized by amperage and rise for exactly this reason, and our <a href="/best-tankless-water-heater/">top tankless water heaters</a> list ranks the gas units by real winter flow rather than the brochure figure.
          </p>

