        <div id="condensing-venting" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Condensing, Venting and Where the Efficiency Comes From
          </h2>

          <p>
            Gas tankless heaters come in two designs. A non-condensing unit has a single heat exchanger, and its exhaust leaves hot, often above 300 degrees. That hot, slightly acidic exhaust needs a stainless steel Category III vent, which is expensive per foot. These models are typically rated around 0.81 to 0.82 UEF, the Uniform Energy Factor the Department of Energy uses to compare water heaters.
          </p>

          <p>
            A condensing unit adds a second heat exchanger that pulls more heat out of the exhaust, cooling it enough that water vapor condenses into liquid. That recovered heat pushes efficiency up to around 0.93 to 0.96 UEF, and the exhaust is cool enough to vent through ordinary PVC pipe. The trade is a condensate line: the liquid is mildly acidic, so it has to run to a drain, usually through a small neutralizer cartridge first.
          </p>

          <p>
            The venting difference often matters more than the efficiency difference. A condensing unit costs more to buy, but cheap PVC venting and more flexible placement can make it the less expensive installation overall. We break the numbers down in <a href="/condensing-vs-non-condensing-tankless-water-heater/">condensing vs non-condensing tankless</a>, including when the condensate drain becomes the deciding problem.
          </p>

          <p>
            Where does the efficiency of tankless come from in the first place? Mostly from eliminating standby loss, the heat a tank leaks through its walls and flue all day while it waits for you. The Department of Energy estimates that for homes using about 41 gallons of hot water a day or less, demand water heaters can be 24 to 34 percent more energy efficient than conventional storage heaters, and 8 to 14 percent more efficient for homes using around 86 gallons a day. Heavy users save less because a tank's standby loss is a smaller share of their total.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">WORTH KNOWING</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Efficiency savings are real but modest in dollars.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">A tank's standby loss is a fixed cost, so the savings are largest in small, low-use households. For a family that already uses a lot of hot water, the bigger advantages are lifespan and endless supply, not the energy bill.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="whole-house-pou" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Whole-House, Point-of-Use and Recirculation
          </h2>

          <p>
            A whole-house tankless heater replaces your tank in the same general location and feeds every fixture through the existing plumbing. A point-of-use tankless heater is a much smaller unit, usually electric, mounted under a sink or near a single shower to serve just that fixture. Point-of-use units draw far less power, and because they sit right next to the tap they deliver hot water almost instantly.
          </p>

          <p>
            Point-of-use heaters are often the right answer for a remote bathroom, a garage sink, a basement bar or an addition that is a long pipe run from the main heater. They can also supplement a tank rather than replace it. Our guide to choosing a <a href="/point-of-use-water-heater/">point of use water heater</a> covers sizing them by fixture and the circuits they need.
          </p>

          <p>
            Recirculation is the other piece. A tankless heater does not shorten the wait for hot water at a distant tap. In fact, the few seconds of ignition add a little to it, because the cold water sitting in the pipes still has to run out first. A recirculation system fixes that by keeping hot water moving through the lines so it is waiting at the fixture when you open it.
          </p>

          <p>
            Some tankless models have a recirculation pump built in and can run on a schedule or learn your usage pattern. Others work with an external pump and either a dedicated return line or a crossover valve under the farthest sink, which uses the cold line as the return. Recirculation costs some energy, so schedules or on-demand buttons are worth using rather than running the loop around the clock.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="day-to-day" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. What Changes Day to Day, and Who It Suits
          </h2>

          <p>
            Living with a tankless heater mostly feels like living with a tank that never runs out. A few quirks are new. If someone turns a tap off and on again quickly, a slug of cooler water can pass through before the burner relights, the so-called cold-water sandwich. Very low flows, like a trickle to rinse a razor, may not reach the activation threshold at all, so the water stays cold until you open the tap further.
          </p>

          <p>
            Maintenance changes too. There is no anode rod to swap and no tank to drain, but minerals in hard water build up on the hot surfaces of the heat exchanger. Most manufacturers call for flushing the unit with a descaling solution every year or so, more often with hard water, and skipping it is the most common reason units fail early. Our guide on <a href="/how-to-flush-a-tankless-water-heater/">how to flush a tankless water heater</a> shows the pump-and-bucket method with isolation valves.
          </p>

          <p>
            Power outages matter more than with an old atmospheric gas tank. Gas tankless units need electricity for the fan, igniter and controls, so no power means no hot water unless you have a backup source. Outdoor models also rely on electric freeze-protection heaters, and they can be damaged if the power fails during a hard freeze without the unit being drained.
          </p>

          <p>
            The payoff is lifespan and space. The Department of Energy notes that most tankless heaters have a life expectancy of more than 20 years, against 10 to 15 for a typical tank, and a wall-mounted unit frees up the closet or corner a tank occupied. Whether that justifies a higher upfront bill depends on your house; our full breakdown of <a href="/tankless-water-heater-pros-and-cons/">tankless water heater pros and cons</a> weighs it honestly, and the <a href="/tankless-water-heater-cost/">tankless water heater cost</a> guide shows the installed numbers.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you have decided to switch, our walkthrough on how to <a href="/replace-water-heater-with-tankless/">replace a water heater with tankless</a> covers the gas line, venting and electrical work the swap involves, and our rundown of <a href="/water-heater-brands/">water heater brands</a> explains who makes what and where each one is sold. When you are ready to choose a unit, start with our <a href="/best-tankless-water-heater/">tested tankless water heater rankings</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Trusting the GPM on the box</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The headline flow rate is quoted at a small temperature rise. In a cold climate the same unit may deliver half that in winter. Size against your coldest incoming water temperature, not the number printed on the packaging.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Assuming electric can heat a whole cold-climate house</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Even the largest residential electric tankless units produce well under half the heat of a big gas model. In northern states, an electric whole-house unit usually manages about one shower at a time in winter, and needs a large share of your panel to do it.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Expecting tankless to shorten the wait for hot water</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The delay at a far tap comes from cold water sitting in the pipes, and a tankless heater does nothing about that. If waiting is the complaint, recirculation or a point-of-use heater at that fixture is the fix.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping the annual flush</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Scale on the heat exchanger reduces output, triggers error codes and shortens the life of the unit. Have isolation valves installed with the heater so a yearly descale takes twenty minutes and a bucket rather than a service call.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring venting and gas-line costs</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The unit price is often the smaller part of the bill. A bigger gas line, a new vent run and a condensate drain can each add hundreds of dollars. Get a full installed quote before choosing a model on purchase price alone.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is a tankless water heater in simple terms?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It is a water heater that does not store hot water. A powerful gas burner or electric elements heat water only while it flows through the unit, so you get hot water for as long as you need it, but only up to a fixed number of gallons per minute at any one time.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How does a tankless water heater know when to turn on?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A flow sensor on the cold inlet detects water moving through the unit. Once flow passes the activation threshold, typically around 0.4 to 0.5 gallons per minute on whole-house gas models, the control board lights the burner or powers the elements, then adjusts heat output to hold your set temperature.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a tankless water heater the same as an on-demand water heater?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. Tankless, on-demand, instant and instantaneous water heater all describe the same design: water is heated as it passes through rather than kept hot in storage. Some marketing uses instant hot water to mean a recirculation system, which is a separate add-on that keeps pipes primed.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do tankless water heaters give instant hot water?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Not at the tap, usually. The heater makes hot water within seconds, but the cold water already in the pipes still has to run out first, so the wait at a distant fixture is about the same as with a tank. Recirculation or a point-of-use heater removes that wait.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can one tankless water heater run two showers at once?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A correctly sized whole-house gas unit usually can, even in colder regions. A 199,000 BTU model delivers around 5 to 6 GPM at a 70-degree rise, enough for two low-flow showers. Most electric whole-house units cannot manage two showers in a cold-climate winter.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does a tankless water heater work during a power outage?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Electric units do not, and most gas units do not either, because the fan, igniter and controls need electricity. A small battery backup or a generator outlet can keep a gas unit running, since its electrical draw is modest compared with the gas it burns.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long does a tankless water heater last?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The Department of Energy puts the life expectancy of most tankless units at more than 20 years, compared with roughly 10 to 15 years for a storage tank. Reaching that figure depends heavily on regular descaling, especially in hard-water areas, and on heat exchanger quality.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

